<BaseLayout title="Research — Hyungwoo Song" lang="en"><section class="page-hero"><div class="wrap"><div class="eyebrow">Research</div><h1 class="page-title">My research follows moments when people hesitate in front of AI.</h1><p class="lead">Accessibility, healthcare, education, and information access may look like separate domains, but for me they return to one question: can people remain interpreters of their own lives even when systems offer help?</p></div></section><section class="section tight"><div class="wrap research-principles natural"><article><span>01</span><h2>People who cannot ask</h2><p>In hospitals, classrooms, and institutions, people often swallow the questions they need to ask. I study interfaces that can reduce that silence.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h2>Voices that count</h2><p>When AI edits speech or writing, I ask whose expression survives and what gets quietly removed.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h2>Support, not substitution</h2><p>Good support does not only finish a task; it can help people understand the situation better.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h2>Design in real settings</h2><p>I look at how technologies are misunderstood, adapted, and negotiated in classrooms, clinics, workplaces, and online information environments.</p></article></div></section><section class="section"><div class="wrap grid two"><article class="card"><span class="stamp">accessibility</span><h3>Accessibility and inclusive interaction</h3><p>Projects such as EmoBridge, AltAuthor, and AltCAT study accessibility across classrooms, screen readers, and cultural contexts.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="stamp">ai agents</span><h3>Human-centered AI and information access</h3><p>AI shopping agents, argument search, and review experiences reveal how AI shapes interpretation and choice.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="stamp">healthcare</span><h3>Healthcare and social adaptation</h3><p>MediBridge-related work asks what people cannot ask, and what support is needed in unfamiliar institutional and healthcare journeys.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="stamp">learning</span><h3>Learning and reflection</h3><p>In classrooms, academic reading, and code comprehension, I study whether AI can scaffold thought rather than only accelerate answers.</p></article></div></section></BaseLayout>